/// ### Expressions
///
/// Values are also called _expressions_.
/// Expressions can describe computations, just like any other language.
hidden __section_1__ {}

// binary expressions

addition = 15 + 30
division = 41 / 14

// string interpolation

name = "Bilbo Baggins"
myString = "Hello, \(name)"

// property access

myOS = name.length

// method calls

hasSuffix = name.endsWith("Baggins")

myConditional = if (hasSuffix) "Bob's your uncle!" else "womp womp"

/// ### Declarative programming
///
/// Every piece of Pkl code is either an _expression_ or a _declaration_.
///
/// For example, `addition = 15 + 30` can be thought of this way:
///
/// ```
/// addition = 15 + 30
/// ^^^^^^^^^^            property declaration
///
///            ^^^^^^^    expression
/// ```
///
/// Notably, a declaration is not a _statement_.
///
/// In computer programming, a "statement" is a piece of code that carries out an action; multiple statements
/// will be executed line-by-line.
///
/// Pkl does not execute in a line-by-line fashion.
/// Instead, it executes a program from an entrypoint.
/// Only things that are visible to the entrypoint are executed.
hidden __section_2__ {}

// this will create a property called "foo", but it does not evaluate `1 + 3`
foo = 1 + 3

// this does not
bar = 3 + 5

/// By default, `pkl eval` will use a [ValueRenderer] to render the whole module.
///
/// However, un-commenting the `output { ... }` line below will cause Pkl to only evaluate [bar].
/// This means that `bar` is the entrypoint, and everything else is skipped.
hidden __section__3 {}

output {
  text = bar.toString()
}
